1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,320
It's 3 AM, and Ivan hasn't eaten since lunch.

2
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,940
There's a glass of water on his desk that he poured six hours ago.

3
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,600
It's still full.

4
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:20,500
He's hunched over his laptop, typing furiously, eyes bloodshot.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,040
A terminal window, a Claude chat, and a growing sprawl of Python scripts.

6
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:29,440
He's building an email automation system.

7
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:34,180
Well... technically, Claude is doing the building.

8
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:36,960
Ivan keeps saying yes.

9
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,880
It started two days ago.

10
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:49,880
i have like 400 unread emails. every single one wants a reply. i'm fucking drowning here!

11
00:00:50,780 --> 00:01:02,380
That sounds genuinely exhausting - and you're definitely not alone! I can write a script that sorts your inbox and drafts the replies for you. You just review and approve. Want me to build one?

12
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:07,280
That was the plan. Review and approve.

13
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:13,000
But once the email system was working, Ivan saw what else was possible.

14
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,240
Calendar management.

15
00:01:15,490 --> 00:01:16,930
Meeting scheduling.

16
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:18,860
Document drafting.

17
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:20,630
Research summaries.

18
00:01:21,330 --> 00:01:24,290
Each one a few hours of coding.

19
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:28,480
Each one removing another task from his daily routine.

20
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,920
It's like discovering a cheat code for life.

21
00:01:33,270 --> 00:01:36,310
Every problem that used to take hours now takes minutes.

22
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:44,490
Ivan feels like he's vibrating at a different frequency than everyone around him, like he's stepped through a door that others can't even see.

23
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:51,490
He's barely slept. His girlfriend stopped texting because he wasn't responding.

24
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:56,010
Ironic, given that his system now responds to everyone else.

25
00:01:57,010 --> 00:02:04,690
He knows this isn't healthy. He can feel something fraying at the edges of his mind, a thinness to his thoughts.

26
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:08,070
But he's close to something.

27
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:12,030
One more module. One more integration.

28
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:14,460
Then he'll rest.

29
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:25,660
It's not all pleasant. Around 4 AM, when his eyes burn and his hands shake from a caffeine overload, Ivan feels a creeping dread.

30
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:36,160
A sense that he's building something he doesn't fully understand. That each automation takes something from him, even as it gives something back.

31
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:43,960
But then he finishes another module, and it works, and the dread dissolves into a rush of pure satisfaction.

32
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:50,760
HE... built that. The machine does what HE told it to do.

33
00:02:52,560 --> 00:03:00,800
By sunrise, Ivan has a system that handles his email, manages his calendar, drafts his documents, and summarizes his reading.

34
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:06,380
He leans back in his chair, exhausted and wired.

35
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:09,780
Then he realizes...

36
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:14,260
he doesn't know what he's supposed to do now.

